Task 1: Environment Setup and Vault Installation
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install Dependencies
First, update your system and install the necessary dependencies:

# Update package repositories
sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y

# Install required packages
sudo apt install -y curl wget unzip jq postgresql-client awscli

# Install Docker for running PostgreSQL locally (simulating RDS)
sudo apt install -y docker.io
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
Log out and log back in to apply the Docker group changes:

exit
Log back into your machine and verify Docker installation:

docker --version
Subtask 1.2: Install HashiCorp Vault
Download and install the latest version of HashiCorp Vault:

# Create a directory for Vault
mkdir -p ~/vault-lab
cd ~/vault-lab

# Download Vault (check for latest version at releases.hashicorp.com)
wget https://releases.hashicorp.com/vault/1.15.2/vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip

# Unzip and install Vault
unzip vault_1.15.2_linux_amd64.zip
sudo mv vault /usr/local/bin/
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/vault

# Verify installation
vault version
Subtask 1.3: Configure Vault Server
Create a Vault configuration file:

# Create Vault configuration directory
mkdir -p ~/vault-lab/config

# Create Vault configuration file
cat > ~/vault-lab/config/vault.hcl << 'EOF'
storage "file" {
  path = "/home/ubuntu/vault-lab/data"
}

listener "tcp" {
  address = "127.0.0.1:8200"
  tls_disable = true
}

api_addr = "http://127.0.0.1:8200"
cluster_addr = "https://127.0.0.1:8201"
ui = true
disable_mlock = true
EOF

# Create data directory
mkdir -p ~/vault-lab/data
Subtask 1.4: Start and Initialize Vault
Start the Vault server:

# Start Vault server in the background
cd ~/vault-lab
vault server -config=config/vault.hcl > vault.log 2>&1 &

# Wait for Vault to start
sleep 5

# Set Vault address environment variable
export VAULT_ADDR='http://127.0.0.1:8200'
echo 'export VAULT_ADDR="http://127.0.0.1:8200"' >> ~/.bashrc

# Initialize Vault
vault operator init -key-shares=1 -key-threshold=1 > vault-keys.txt

# Extract unseal key and root token
UNSEAL_KEY=$(grep 'Unseal Key 1:' vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $NF}')
ROOT_TOKEN=$(grep 'Initial Root Token:' vault-keys.txt | awk '{print $NF}')

# Unseal Vault
vault operator unseal $UNSEAL_KEY

# Authenticate with root token
vault auth $ROOT_TOKEN
Verify Vault is running and unsealed:

vault status
Task 2: Set Up Database Environment
Subtask 2.1: Deploy PostgreSQL Database
For this lab, we'll use PostgreSQL in a Docker container to simulate an AWS RDS instance:

# Run PostgreSQL container
docker run --name postgres-db \
  -e POSTGRES_DB=testdb \
  -e POSTGRES_USER=postgres \
  -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=rootpassword \
  -p 5432:5432 \
  -d postgres:13

# Wait for PostgreSQL to start
sleep 10

# Verify PostgreSQL is running
docker ps | grep postgres-db
Subtask 2.2: Configure Database for Dynamic Secrets
Connect to PostgreSQL and create necessary users and permissions:

# Connect to PostgreSQL and create a vault user
docker exec -it postgres-db psql -U postgres -d testdb << 'EOF'
-- Create a user for Vault to manage dynamic users
CREATE USER vault WITH PASSWORD 'vaultpassword';

-- Grant necessary permissions to vault user
ALTER USER vault CREATEDB;
ALTER USER vault CREATEROLE;

-- Create a test table for verification
CREATE TABLE employees (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100),
    department VARCHAR(50)
);

-- Insert sample data
INSERT INTO employees (name, department) VALUES 
('John Doe', 'Engineering'),
('Jane Smith', 'Marketing'),
('Bob Johnson', 'Sales');

-- Grant permissions on the table
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO vault;
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON ALL SEQUENCES IN SCHEMA public TO vault;

\q
EOF
Verify the database setup:

# Test connection with vault user
PGPASSWORD=vaultpassword psql -h localhost -U vault -d testdb -c "SELECT * FROM employees;"
Task 3: Configure Vault Database Secrets Engine
Subtask 3.1: Enable Database Secrets Engine
Enable and configure the database secrets engine in Vault:

# Enable the database secrets engine
vault secrets enable database

# Verify the secrets engine is enabled
vault secrets list
Subtask 3.2: Configure Database Connection
Configure Vault to connect to your PostgreSQL database:

# Configure database connection
vault write database/config/postgresql \
    plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \
    connection_url="postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@localhost:5432/testdb?sslmode=disable" \
    allowed_roles="readonly,readwrite" \
    username="vault" \
    password="vaultpassword"

# Verify the connection configuration
vault read database/config/postgresql
Subtask 3.3: Create Database Roles
Create different roles for various access levels:

# Create a read-only role
vault write database/roles/readonly \
    db_name=postgresql \
    creation_statements="CREATE ROLE \"{{name}}\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; \
        GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"{{name}}\";" \
    default_ttl="1h" \
    max_ttl="24h"

# Create a read-write role
vault write database/roles/readwrite \
    db_name=postgresql \
    creation_statements="CREATE ROLE \"{{name}}\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; \
        GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"{{name}}\";" \
    default_ttl="1h" \
    max_ttl="24h"

# Verify roles are created
vault list database/roles
vault read database/roles/readonly
vault read database/roles/readwrite
Task 4: Generate and Test Dynamic Secrets
Subtask 4.1: Generate Dynamic Database Credentials
Generate dynamic credentials for different roles:

# Generate read-only credentials
echo "=== Generating Read-Only Credentials ==="
vault read database/creds/readonly

# Store the credentials for testing
READONLY_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/readonly)
READONLY_USER=$(echo $READONLY_CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
READONLY_PASS=$(echo $READONLY_CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')

echo "Read-only Username: $READONLY_USER"
echo "Read-only Password: $READONLY_PASS"

# Generate read-write credentials
echo "=== Generating Read-Write Credentials ==="
vault read database/creds/readwrite

# Store the credentials for testing
READWRITE_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/readwrite)
READWRITE_USER=$(echo $READWRITE_CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
READWRITE_PASS=$(echo $READWRITE_CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')

echo "Read-write Username: $READWRITE_USER"
echo "Read-write Password: $READWRITE_PASS"
Subtask 4.2: Test Read-Only Access
Test the read-only credentials:

echo "=== Testing Read-Only Access ==="

# Test SELECT operation (should work)
PGPASSWORD=$READONLY_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READONLY_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT * FROM employees;"

# Test INSERT operation (should fail)
echo "Attempting INSERT with read-only user (should fail):"
PGPASSWORD=$READONLY_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READONLY_USER -d testdb -c "INSERT INTO employees (name, department) VALUES ('Test User', 'Test Dept');" 2>&1 || echo "INSERT failed as expected for read-only user"
Subtask 4.3: Test Read-Write Access
Test the read-write credentials:

echo "=== Testing Read-Write Access ==="

# Test SELECT operation (should work)
PGPASSWORD=$READWRITE_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READWRITE_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT * FROM employees;"

# Test INSERT operation (should work)
echo "Attempting INSERT with read-write user (should succeed):"
PGPASSWORD=$READWRITE_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READWRITE_USER -d testdb -c "INSERT INTO employees (name, department) VALUES ('Dynamic User', 'Test Department');"

# Verify the insert worked
PGPASSWORD=$READWRITE_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READWRITE_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT * FROM employees WHERE name = 'Dynamic User';"

# Test UPDATE operation (should work)
PGPASSWORD=$READWRITE_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READWRITE_USER -d testdb -c "UPDATE employees SET department = 'Updated Department' WHERE name = 'Dynamic User';"

# Verify the update worked
PGPASSWORD=$READWRITE_PASS psql -h localhost -U $READWRITE_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT * FROM employees WHERE name = 'Dynamic User';"
Task 5: Demonstrate Dynamic Secret Lifecycle
Subtask 5.1: Monitor Active Leases
Check active leases for dynamic secrets:

# List all active leases
vault list sys/leases/lookup/database/creds/readonly
vault list sys/leases/lookup/database/creds/readwrite

# Get detailed information about a specific lease
echo "=== Lease Information ==="
vault write sys/leases/lookup lease_id=$(vault read -field=lease_id database/creds/readonly)
Subtask 5.2: Renew Dynamic Secrets
Demonstrate lease renewal:

# Generate new credentials and capture lease ID
NEW_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/readonly)
LEASE_ID=$(echo $NEW_CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')
NEW_USER=$(echo $NEW_CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
NEW_PASS=$(echo $NEW_CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')

echo "New Lease ID: $LEASE_ID"
echo "New Username: $NEW_USER"

# Test the credentials work
PGPASSWORD=$NEW_PASS psql -h localhost -U $NEW_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees;"

# Renew the lease
echo "=== Renewing Lease ==="
vault write sys/leases/renew lease_id=$LEASE_ID increment=3600

# Check lease information after renewal
vault write sys/leases/lookup lease_id=$LEASE_ID
Subtask 5.3: Revoke Dynamic Secrets
Demonstrate secret revocation:

# Test that credentials work before revocation
echo "=== Testing Credentials Before Revocation ==="
PGPASSWORD=$NEW_PASS psql -h localhost -U $NEW_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT 'Credentials work before revocation' as status;"

# Revoke the lease
echo "=== Revoking Lease ==="
vault write sys/leases/revoke lease_id=$LEASE_ID

# Wait a moment for revocation to take effect
sleep 5

# Test that credentials no longer work after revocation
echo "=== Testing Credentials After Revocation (should fail) ==="
PGPASSWORD=$NEW_PASS psql -h localhost -U $NEW_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT 'This should fail' as status;" 2>&1 || echo "Credentials successfully revoked - access denied as expected"

# Verify user was removed from database
docker exec -it postgres-db psql -U postgres -d testdb -c "SELECT usename FROM pg_user WHERE usename = '$NEW_USER';"
Task 6: Advanced Dynamic Secrets Configuration
Subtask 6.1: Configure Custom TTL and Rotation
Create a role with custom TTL settings:

# Create a role with shorter TTL for demonstration
vault write database/roles/short-lived \
    db_name=postgresql \
    creation_statements="CREATE ROLE \"{{name}}\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}'; \
        GRANT SELECT ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO \"{{name}}\";" \
    default_ttl="5m" \
    max_ttl="10m"

# Generate credentials with short TTL
echo "=== Generating Short-Lived Credentials ==="
SHORT_CREDS=$(vault read -format=json database/creds/short-lived)
SHORT_USER=$(echo $SHORT_CREDS | jq -r '.data.username')
SHORT_PASS=$(echo $SHORT_CREDS | jq -r '.data.password')
SHORT_LEASE=$(echo $SHORT_CREDS | jq -r '.lease_id')

echo "Short-lived Username: $SHORT_USER"
echo "Lease Duration: $(echo $SHORT_CREDS | jq -r '.lease_duration') seconds"

# Test the credentials
PGPASSWORD=$SHORT_PASS psql -h localhost -U $SHORT_USER -d testdb -c "SELECT 'Short-lived credentials work' as status;"
Subtask 6.2: Bulk Revocation
Demonstrate bulk revocation of secrets:

# Generate multiple credentials
echo "=== Generating Multiple Credentials for Bulk Revocation ==="
for i in {1..3}; do
    vault read database/creds/readonly > /dev/null
    echo "Generated credential set $i"
done

# List all active leases
echo "=== Active Leases Before Bulk Revocation ==="
vault list sys/leases/lookup/database/creds/readonly

# Revoke all leases for the readonly role
echo "=== Performing Bulk Revocation ==="
vault write sys/leases/revoke-prefix prefix=database/creds/readonly

# Verify leases are revoked
echo "=== Active Leases After Bulk Revocation ==="
vault list sys/leases/lookup/database/creds/readonly 2>/dev/null || echo "No active leases found - bulk revocation successful"
